# Twin remotes — dual push

This workspace has **two** GitHub remotes for the same codebase. Treat them as twins: every published commit should land on **both** unless the user explicitly says otherwise.

## Remotes

| Remote | Repo | Who |
|---|---|---|
| `origin` | https://github.com/izoo2003/Kafi-HR-Agent | Personal (`izoo2003`) |
| `kafi` | https://github.com/kafi-group/Kafi-Admin-HR-Agent | Org (`kafi-group`) |

Verify with `git remote -v` if unsure. Do not invent other remote names.

## When the user asks to push

Triggers include: "push", "push both", "push to both repos", "publish", "twin push".

1. Commit only if they asked to commit (existing commit rules still apply).
2. Push the same branch to **both** remotes **from the terminal / Shell tool** (never ask the user to open GitHub Desktop or push manually unless auth truly blocks the CLI):
   - `git push origin HEAD`
   - `git push kafi HEAD`
3. If one push fails (auth/403), report which remote failed and do not claim both succeeded. Tell them which GitHub account the CLI is using if the error names one (e.g. denied to `kafi-group` when pushing `origin`).
4. Do **not** force-push either remote unless the user explicitly requests it.
5. After both succeed, confirm both remote `main` SHAs match local `HEAD`.

## Deploy / URL notes (do not mix up)

- Live frontend: `https://kafi-admin-hr-agent.vercel.app` (often wired to the **org** repo).
- Live API: `https://kafi-hr-agent-production.up.railway.app`
- Old host `kafi-hr-agent.up.railway.app` is stale — do not point the frontend at it.

## Do not

- Push only to `origin` and leave `kafi` behind (or the reverse) when the user asked for a normal publish/push.
- Assume Vercel/Railway redeployed from the remote you did not push to.
- Commit secrets (`.env`, credentials) to either repo.
